[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So, factually: **`normalize` performs no validation whatsoever.** There is no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `RETURN_ERROR` macro, no `return -1`, no `return NULL`, no error enum, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `assert`, no null check, no range check, and no min/max constant. The function
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns `void`, so it has no error channel at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The rejection surface is therefore made of the *implicit* rejections — the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `if` conditions above — plus the boundary/degenerate inputs the C accepts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m silently and the inputs on which the C traps. "Expected C result" below is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observable effect (bytes written / signal raised), because there is no return
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reference implementation (ground truth):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void normalize(float *dest, const float *src, int size) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     float sum = 0.0f;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     int i;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     for (i = 0; i < size; i++)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         sum += src[i] * src[i];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     if (sum > 0.0f) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         sum = 1.0f / sqrtf(sum);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         for (i = 0; i < size; i++)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m             dest[i] = src[i] * sum;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     } else if (dest != src) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         memset(dest, 0, size * sizeof(float));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two facts drive most rows:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `sum > 0.0f` is **false** for `sum == +0.0` and for `sum == NaN`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (unordered compare). Those inputs fall into the `else if`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `size * sizeof(float)` promotes `int size` to `size_t` by the usual
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   arithmetic conversions, so a negative `size` becomes a near-`2^64` byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   count.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The table
