[49m[38;5;10m+     1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # CONFIGS.md — configuration surface table (Phase A, gate for Phase B)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Axes derived from the C source
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There is exactly one public entry point, and it is also the lowest-level one:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void normalize(float *dest, const float *src, int size);   /* c_src/include/lib.h */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m There are no convenience wrappers, no init/teardown, no opaque context, and no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m higher layer to compose — the whole library is this one call, so "driving it the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m way a real consumer does" *is* calling `normalize` directly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Runtime options/modes/flags: **none**. Verified mechanically:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m grep -nE '#ifdef|#if |switch|enum|typedef|extern' -r c_src/src c_src/include   # no match
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the configuration axes are the *input shapes* the code branches on. The C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m has exactly three branch points, and they define the axes:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `A. size` — controls both loop trip counts (`i < size`) and the `memset`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   length (`size * sizeof(float)`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Values: `0`, `1`, `2`, `3`, `4`, `5`, `7`, `8`, `15`, `16`, `17`, `31`, `33`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `64`, `1000`, `4096`, `65536` (odd/even, powers of two ±1, "empty / one /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   many"), plus the negative and `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` cases which live in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ERRORS.md`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `B. pointer relationship` — the `dest != src` test, and the read-then-write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ordering of the second loop.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Values: `disjoint` · `identical (dest == src, in-place)` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `forward overlap (dest == src + 1)` · `backward overlap (dest == src - 1)` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `partial overlap (dest == src + size/2)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `C. the class of `sum`` — the `sum > 0.0f` test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Values: `positive normal` · `exactly +0.0 (all inputs zero)` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `exactly +0.0 by underflow (non-zero inputs whose squares round to 0)` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `positive denormal` · `+inf (overflow)` · `NaN (NaN input)` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `+inf via ±inf input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `D. element value population` — value-dependent rounding in the accumulation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and in `src[i] * sum`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Values: `uniform [-1,1]` · `wide exponent range` · `all denormal` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `mixed denormal + normal` · `near FLT_MAX` · `±0.0 mixed` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `±inf sprinkled` · `NaN sprinkled` · `exact powers of two` ·
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `sum contrived to be exactly 1.0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rows below are the cross-product **pruned to the combinations the C actually
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinguishes**: axis C × axis B is the real decision table (which of the three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m code paths runs, and whether the write is even performed), and axes A × D are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m swept randomly inside every row.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Every row is driven with **many randomized inputs** from a seeded xorshift64\*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m PRNG (`SEED = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15`, fixed → reproducible), not a single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hand-picked value. Each iteration re-randomises `size` (from axis A), the
